WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.680
Water holds far less oxygen than air.

2
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.480
Yet a trout's gill can bring its blood close to the oxygen partial pressure of the

3
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.240
water flowing in. Your lung cannot do that with air.

4
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.080
Why not?

5
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:23.243
Oxygen and carbon dioxide cross gills and lungs by diffusion.

6
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:26.543
They move from higher partial pressure to lower.

7
00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:34.863
Dry air at sea level is about 21% oxygen, at a total pressure of about 760 mmHg.

8
00:00:35.643 --> 00:00:38.683
So oxygen's partial pressure there is roughly 160.

9
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:44.623
The rate rises with area and with the pressure difference, and falls with barrier

10
00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:48.563
thickness. So the question is where that difference can be kept up.

11
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:55.607
In teleost fish, such as trout, each gill carries filaments, and each filament

12
00:00:55.607 --> 00:00:58.147
carries thin plates called lamellae.

13
00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:02.687
Inside a lamella, blood flows opposite to the water.

14
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:05.267
This is countercurrent exchange.

15
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.667
Blood about to leave meets water that has just arrived.

16
00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:13.187
So a pressure difference holds along the whole lamella.

17
00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:18.747
If both streams ran the same way, they would reach equal partial pressure partway

18
00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:21.407
along. The rest of the lamella would add nothing.

19
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.000
Now the mammal lung.

20
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.720
In mammals, air flows in and out through the same airways.

21
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.000
This is called tidal ventilation.

22
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.940
The airways end in tiny dead-ended sacs, the alveoli.

23
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.800
Each breath mixes with stale air left behind.

24
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:47.160
So in an adult human at rest, alveolar oxygen partial pressure is about 100 mmHg.

25
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.500
Warmed, humidified inhaled air is roughly 150.

26
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.400
Blood can at best match the alveolar gas.

27
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:57.913
Birds do it differently.

28
00:01:58.813 --> 00:02:02.653
Their lungs exchange gas in tubes called parabronchi.

29
00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:08.753
Through most of them, air flows the same way during breathing in and breathing out.

30
00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:11.713
Capillaries cross the airstream.

31
00:02:12.413 --> 00:02:14.273
This is crosscurrent exchange.

32
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:18.493
Each stretch of capillary meets air at a different stage.

33
00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:24.973
So mixed blood leaving the lung can hold a higher partial pressure than the air breathed out.

34
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:33.253
In 1972, Johannes Piiper and Peter Scheid compared idealized models of these three

35
00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:35.293
arrangements under matched conditions.

36
00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:39.673
By their measure, countercurrent moved the most gas.

37
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:41.773
Crosscurrent came next.

38
00:02:42.393 --> 00:02:45.553
The mixed pool of a mammal lung moved the least.

39
00:02:46.493 --> 00:02:48.853
That ranks arrangements, not animals.

40
00:02:48.853 --> 00:02:54.953
Keep this rule: how two streams flow past each other changes gas exchange.
